    {
      "id": "Kclp",
      "code_hash": "c9ced16ea132b3ffe1153c8cf8aae71b",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">A recomputed node's change, the difference between its new and its old\nresult, still flows to the nodes that read it; that is how chapters 2\nand 3 maintain a window and an aggregate. The reason <code>status()</code> gives\nis a short, generic one; the list above is the full one.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Nodes are built from Ibis transforms: a view written as a SQL string\nhas no public counterpart yet. And the view is a table like any other:\n<code>view.table</code> is an Ibis table to query further, and <code>view.to_arrow()</code>,\n<code>to_polars()</code> and <code>to_pandas()</code> hand you its rows.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "emfo",
      "code_hash": "858e1b14718f4b296794bd8d9f69b337",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"how-it-works-rows-with-weights\">How it works: rows with weights</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">So how does the view know what changed? The trick that makes it\ntractable is to give every row a <strong>weight</strong>:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><code>+1</code>: this row was inserted,</li>\n<li><code>\u22121</code>: this row was deleted,</li>\n<li>an update is a delete plus an insert.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A table with weights is called a <em>Z-set</em>. Adding a batch of changes\nmeans putting the weighted rows together and summing the weights per\nrow. Here's a small mirror of <code>SubstraitLean/Incremental.lean</code>, and a\nquery over it:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
