    {
      "id": "qnkX",
      "code_hash": "b2b7176ba4879ca045d91d1d14508851",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">For this query the rule is simple: <strong>run the same query on only the\nchanges</strong>, then add the result to the old one. That works because\nfilter and project treat each row independently (they are <em>linear</em>).\nIn Lean that is theorem <code>Rel.zeval_add</code>:</span>\n<div class=\"language-lean codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rel.zeval_add</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r.Incremental</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">\u2192</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r.zeval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">\u0394</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Perm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r.zeval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">++</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r.zdelta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">\u0394</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Read: <em>for every plan in the supported fragment, evaluating on the\nupdated database gives the old result plus the delta (in some order:\n<code>Perm</code> means \"same rows, possibly reordered\").</em></span>\n<h2 id=\"joins-are-trickier\">Joins are trickier</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A join pairs rows from two tables. If <strong>both</strong> tables change, the new\nresult has four kinds of pairs:</span>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">old customers <code>B</code></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">new customers <code>\u0394B</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>old orders <code>A</code></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><code>A \u00d7 B</code>: already in the old result</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><code>A \u00d7 \u0394B</code>: new</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>new orders <code>\u0394A</code></strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><code>\u0394A \u00d7 B</code>: new</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><code>\u0394A \u00d7 \u0394B</code>: new, and easy to forget</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">So the change to a join is <code>\u0394A \u00d7 B + \u0394A \u00d7 \u0394B + A \u00d7 \u0394B</code>. Forgetting the\nbottom-right cell is a classic bug in hand-written incremental\npipelines. Toggle it off to see what goes wrong:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
