    {
      "id": "DnEU",
      "code_hash": "0116e9e4ff6b6c8d10dc394959cd699a",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">With the default changes, turning the switch off breaks the result in\ntwo ways:</span>\n<ul>\n<li>the pair <em>order 4 + customer <code>cy</code></em> is missing. Both are new, so only\n  the corner term produces it;</li>\n<li>phantom <code>bob</code> rows appear, one with weight <strong>\u22121</strong>: a row that \"exists\n  minus one times\". The updated order 2 was paired with the <em>old</em> <code>bob</code>,\n  and only the corner term cancels that pairing with <code>bob</code>'s deletion.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Lean proves the full rule once and for all, and goes one step further:\n<code>Rel.delta</code> <em>generates</em> the update plan automatically, as an ordinary\nplan that reads the old tables and the change tables, and\n<code>Rel.delta_correct</code> proves the generated plan computes exactly the\nchange. Since the update plan is an ordinary plan, any engine can run\nit.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ulZA",
      "code_hash": "fedf37150aeb448e946000471652245a",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"what-runs-on-each-batch\">What runs on each batch</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The three update plans are exactly the three new cells of the join\ntable above, as Lean wrote them; <code>sverify.engines.delta_plans(view.plan)</code>\ngives them to you. <code>orders$delta</code> and\n<code>customers$delta</code> are the change tables, with a trailing <code>__weight</code>\ncolumn; old tables get weight 1 (<code>Project +[1]</code>), and the join\nmultiplies weights (<code>$3 * $6</code>):</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ecfG",
      "code_hash": "b2c349148d60ca10da347b49914e6cb1",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/html": "<div style='display: flex;flex: 1;flex-direction: row;justify-content: flex-start;align-items: normal;flex-wrap: wrap;gap: 0.5rem'><span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><div class=\"language-text codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code>Emit [0, 5, 7]\n  Project +[($3 * $6)]\n    InnerJoin ($1 = $4)\n      Read orders$delta\n      Project +[1]\n        Read customers\n</code></pre></div></span><span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><div class=\"language-text codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code>Emit [0, 5, 7]\n  Project +[($3 * $6)]\n    InnerJoin ($1 = $4)\n      Read orders$delta\n      Read customers$delta\n</code></pre></div></span><span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><div class=\"language-text codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code>Emit [0, 5, 7]\n  Project +[($3 * $6)]\n    InnerJoin ($1 = $4)\n      Project +[1]\n        Read orders\n      Read customers$delta\n</code></pre></div></span></div>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "Pvdt",
      "code_hash": "c7f55d86477c5d5014dd24063fc7c8dc",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">Step by step:</span>\n<ol>\n<li>The transform becomes a Substrait plan (sverify's Ibis producer);\n   <code>view.plan</code> is that plan as Lean checked and wrote it.</li>\n<li><code>substrait-check --delta</code> builds the update plan (<code>Rel.delta</code>),\n   splits it into pieces without <code>UNION ALL</code> (<code>Rel.terms</code>), and encodes\n   each row's weight as the <code>__weight</code> column (<code>Rel.weighted</code>). Each step\n   has a theorem saying it computes exactly the change to the result;\n   <code>Rel.weighted_eval</code> covers running it on an ordinary engine.</li>\n<li>On each refresh, <code>sverify</code> registers the change tables, runs the\n   plans, sums the weights per row, and merges the result into the\n   view's state.</li>\n</ol>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A batch is computed completely before anything is replaced, so if\nanything fails, nothing changes. A change that can't happen at all,\nsuch as deleting a row that doesn't exist, doesn't even get that far:\nthe source refuses it, and neither <code>orders</code> nor the view moves.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ZBYS",
      "code_hash": "5cd9c8406ca152670bfe2c2f6a432d98",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/html": "<marimo-callout-output data-html='&quot;&#92;u003cspan class=&#92;&quot;markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents&#92;&quot;&#92;u003e&#92;u003cspan class=&#92;&quot;paragraph&#92;&quot;&#92;u003e&#92;u003cstrong&#92;u003eValueError&#92;u003c/strong&#92;u003e: cannot delete (99, &#x27;nobody&#x27;, 1) from orders: no such row. &#92;u003ccode&#92;u003eorders&#92;u003c/code&#92;u003e is still at version 0, and the view still reflects {&#x27;orders&#x27;: 0, &#x27;customers&#x27;: 0}: True.&#92;u003c/span&#92;u003e&#92;u003c/span&#92;u003e&quot;' data-kind='&quot;warn&quot;'></marimo-callout-output>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "aLJB",
      "code_hash": "0a34f4fa5aa69862761ffed9da57bd58",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h3 id=\"how-do-i-know-its-right\">How do I know it's right?</h3>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Before you point a dashboard at it, you'll want more than a demo. The\ntheorems cover the Lean model. The rest (the JSON translation, the\nPython glue, the engine itself) is covered by randomized tests: they\ngenerate random tables and random batches, with NULLs and duplicates,\nand after every batch compare the maintained view with a full\nrecompute, as the button above does. In production you can ask for\nthe same check on every batch: <code>view.refresh(audit=True)</code> compares the\nbatch with recomputation before applying it, and raises <code>AuditError</code>\n(from <code>sverify.errors</code>) instead of applying a wrong one.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">You can now keep a join up to date from its changes alone, with update\nrules proved in Lean, and you know which queries are recomputed rather\nthan maintained wrongly. Next up, <a href=\"?file=10_pipelines.py\">Pipelines</a>\nstacks transforms into layers, stores the ones others read, so each\nchange flows through every layer, and brings back the contracts of\nchapter 8.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
